{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Summary\n\n`list_tickets` against this store can terminate with `MCP request failed: Transport closed` and\nno structured error payload — the caller cannot tell whether the query was invalid, the server\nfailed, or the response was simply too large.\n\n## PROVEN root cause (Phase 1, 2026-08-28)\n\nUnfiltered `tools/call list_tickets {}` against `https://rebar.solutions.navateam.com/mcp`\n(Streamable HTTP, bearer auth) returned `http=200`, **94,541,551 bytes over 177.29 seconds**, in\none well-formed SSE `event: message` frame. **The server does not fail.** The CLIENT abandons a\n94.5 MB / 177 s single response, and how it abandons is client-specific: Copilot CLI reports\n`Transport closed`; a Claude Code session with a 30 s connect budget times out; a third client\nsucceeded but spilled 219,815 chars to disk. None can distinguish \"too big\" from \"server died\",\nbecause the server offers no bound and no structured signal.\n\n## Reconciliation with `unreal-milky-sloth` (`6fe7-956f-4901-45cf`) — read this before fixing\n\nThat sibling **REFUTED the response-size hypothesis, and was right to on its evidence**: its\nfailing call was 50,532 bytes while another client succeeded at ~219,000 (\"size does not\ndiscriminate\"); its symptom was `TypeError: Do not know how to serialize a BigInt`, a type error,\nnot a size or timeout error; and post-fix it live-validated `ready_tickets` returning 939,719\ncharacters successfully.\n\nBoth findings stand, because they are about **different failures**:\n\n* `unreal-milky-sloth`: the server emitted bare 19-digit integers and a JS client threw while\n  parsing. Fixed and live-validated; `js_safe_result` now quotes them.\n* **this ticket**: measured **after** that fix shipped, the payload parses cleanly — and is\n  94.5 MB. That is not a serialization defect; it is an unbounded payload.\n\nTherefore the **219,815-character datum is NOT evidence for this ticket's cause and is not used\nas such.** At 4.3x the size of a failing call it discriminates nothing, exactly as the sibling\nargued. The discriminating evidence is the 94.5 MB / 177 s measurement taken post-fix, which\nBigInt cannot explain. The other two original candidates are closed: BigInt **REJECTED** (the\ncall completes and parses); deploy cutover **REJECTED** (reproducible on demand three days after\nthe 18:54:29Z container replacement, from a session that was never orphaned).\n\n## The guard that already exists, and why it is not enough\n\nThis is **not greenfield**. `list_tickets` is already lean-by-default (`full: bool = False`),\ndropping `description` and `comments`. Measured on this store (2,855 tickets) that saves 39%\n(73.0 MB full -> 44.4 MB lean) and leaves the bulk untouched: of the **lean** payload,\n`authorship_ledger` is 59%, `attestations` 23%, `signature` 6% — **88% is base64 DSSE signature\nmaterial** no list caller consumes. Per row: 15,548 bytes lean vs 1,726 with those four (plus\n`keyring`) dropped. `ready_tickets` has **no lean mode and no limit at all** (61 rows, 693,556\nbytes). The one payload bound that does exist — `_cap_workflow_payload`, 90,000 bytes, whose\ncomment states \"under the client's ~25K-token budget\" — is wired only to `get_workflow_status`\nand `get_workflow_result`.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [x] The cause is identified and explicitly reconciled with both sibling tickets (above).\n- [ ] `list_tickets` AND `ready_tickets` are bounded at the same **90,000-byte**\n      `_WORKFLOW_TOKEN_BUDGET_BYTES` the workflow cap already enforces, measured on the payload\n      the client actually receives (post-`model_validate`, post-`js_safe_result`), not on the raw\n      reducer rows.\n- [ ] Exceeding that budget produces a structured error with `error_code=\"response_too_large\"`\n      naming the match count, the byte size, the budget, and the filters to narrow with. Never a\n      partial list (a short list cannot be told from a complete one); never a bare transport close.\n- [ ] The lean projection drops `authorship_ledger`, `attestations`, `signature` and `keyring` as\n      well as the two bodies, through one function shared by both read backends, with `full=True`\n      restoring the previous bytes exactly. Any surface whose default output narrows as a result\n      is recorded as a pre-1.0 breaking change in `docs/release-notes.md` and `CHANGELOG.md`.\n- [ ] Regression coverage exercises both edges: a synthetic store of >=4,000 rows (over budget)\n      is refused, and an under-budget list is returned whole and unmodified.\n\n- [ ] **LIVE VALIDATION — REQUIRED TO CLOSE.** `list_tickets` and `ready_tickets` called through\n      the deployed endpoint return results (or a structured limit error) with no transport close.\n      **Auth precondition:** `https://rebar.solutions.navateam.com/mcp` is behind the OAuth gate,\n      so the validating client MUST present a valid OAuth bearer token; a `401`/`403` is a FAILED\n      validation, not an environment excuse. Evidence must be OBSERVED against that deployed\n      server and recorded in a ticket comment naming the tool(s) called, the arguments, and the\n      actual response. A passing local test is NOT enough, and neither is a merged change: the\n      container must have redeployed and served the call. This exists because a fix here was\n      previously reported complete on the strength of a merge while the deployed behaviour was\n      still broken."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1787951959802377001,"uuid":"9cb6e180-30f3-4417-824e-83decbc89870"}